WEBVTT

0
00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:15.250
Respect the guests themselves.

1
00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:17.250
Compared with many other programmes, the editing

2
00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:18.250
can be very inventive.

3
00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:20.250
The company’s working environment,

4
00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:21.250
or the atmosphere at work—

5
00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:22.250
what impression did it leave?

6
00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:23.250
So good. Really good.

7
00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:25.250
The producers and guests

8
00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:27.250
help one another develop.

9
00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:31.250
I think their reality shows,

10
00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:32.250
in a sense,

11
00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:33.250
are more like documentaries.

12
00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:34.250
Documentaries.

13
00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:36.250
They feel less artificial,

14
00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:37.250
without deliberately manufacturing conflict

15
00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:38.250
or designing a plot.

16
00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.250
For example, Happy Friends:

17
00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:42.250
we see a group of good friends

18
00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:44.250
playing games together.

19
00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:46.250
They are not deliberately trying to satisfy

20
00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:47.250
the production team’s requirements.

21
00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:49.250
And Become a Farmer:

22
00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:51.250
that may lean more towards a carefully crafted programme.

23
00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:52.250
It feels very real.

24
00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:55.250
Ten young men genuinely work the land.

25
00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:56.250
We can clearly see

26
00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:57.250
them encounter difficulties,

27
00:00:57.250 --> 00:00:58.250
ask different people for help,

28
00:00:58.250 --> 00:00:59.250
learn,

29
00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:01.250
and keep growing through the process.

30
00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:03.250
I am Zeng Yanyu.

31
00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:07.250
From July 2021 to January 2022,

32
00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:08.250
I was an assistant editing intern at Daqian in Hangzhou.

33
00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.250
Daqian’s working environment,

34
00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:11.250
or workplace atmosphere—

35
00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:12.250
what impression did it leave?

36
00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:13.250
So good. Really good.

37
00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:15.250
It was wonderful and comfortable.

38
00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:16.250
I was only a junior editor.

39
00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:17.250
Everyone

40
00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:18.250
had a computer tower, desk and monitor.

41
00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:19.250
I noticed

42
00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:20.250
that colleagues who had worked there a long time

43
00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.250
had desks covered with cuddly toys.

44
00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:23.250
They had

45
00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:24.250
decorated their workstations as they liked,

46
00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:25.250
in a very cute way.

47
00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:28.250
The workstations formed a square,

48
00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:29.250
with five or six computers,

49
00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.250
and an area in the middle where anyone could lie down.

50
00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:32.250
When someone had finished editing,

51
00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:34.250
there was this enormous

52
00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:35.250
toy bear.

53
00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:37.250
Anyone could lie down there for a while,

54
00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:39.250
then get up again after resting.

55
00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:40.250
That is something about Daqian.

56
00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:41.250
It really is a versatile studio setup.

57
00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:43.250
First, the equipment was excellent.

58
00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:45.250
Everyone needed two monitors,

59
00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:47.250
one landscape and one portrait.

60
00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:48.250
It was a flexible arrangement.

61
00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:49.250
You could work across both monitors,

62
00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:51.250
moving between the two.

63
00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:52.250
I do not know how it was configured.

64
00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:54.250
The landscape screen showed the picture;

65
00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:55.250
the portrait screen showed editing tracks vertically.

66
00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:57.250
Editing involves many tracks,

67
00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:58.250
nearly forty at a time,

68
00:01:58.250 --> 00:01:59.250
which need a long vertical space.

69
00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.250
That is why the second screen was upright.

70
00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:03.250
This editing equipment

71
00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:05.250
made the work much easier.

72
00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:07.250
When I returned to editing on my own laptop,

73
00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:08.250
it felt wrong.

74
00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:09.250
I wanted to adjust the workspace

75
00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:10.250
to look exactly like Daqian’s.

76
00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:12.250
And then,

77
00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:14.250
the everyday environment:

78
00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:15.250
as I just said,

79
00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:16.250
every editor decorated their own area,

80
00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:17.250
making it comfortable

81
00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:18.250
with little ornaments.

82
00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:20.250
There were sports facilities too,

83
00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:21.250
a gym.

84
00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:23.250
Because Shining Days

85
00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:26.250
focuses on the lives of young urban people—

86
00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:27.250
I forget the exact description—

87
00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:29.250
what it shows

88
00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:30.250
is the daily life of some not particularly famous

89
00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:31.250
young celebrities.

90
00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:32.250
But their daily lives are not

91
00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:33.250
too far from ours.

92
00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:34.250
You can actually see

93
00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:35.250
reflections of your own life.

94
00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:37.250
That makes it feel very real.

95
00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:38.250
That is the feeling.

96
00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:39.250
They are flexible,

97
00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:41.250
using social media, for example.

98
00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:43.250
And here, they are also quite…

99
00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:44.250
In the Internet era, everyone can leave comments

100
00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:45.250
and express opinions.

101
00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:46.250
During that process,

102
00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:47.250
they listen to these voices.

103
00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:49.250
Those responses

104
00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:50.250
may inspire their next release,

105
00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:51.250
or

106
00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:52.250
appear in later content

107
00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:53.250
as a response to viewers.

108
00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.250
They previously released merchandise,

109
00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:56.250
a Shining Days bag.

110
00:02:57.250 --> 00:02:59.250
They have actually made quite a lot of merchandise.

111
00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:00.250
Starting with season five,

112
00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:01.250
they first opened

113
00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:02.250
an offline café in Beijing.

114
00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:04.250
Quite a lot of people visited it.

115
00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.250
Later they sold merchandise online.

116
00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:09.250
I bought one of those bags then.

117
00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:10.250
As I said, it is a show to watch over a meal.

118
00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:12.250
By the time you finish eating, the programme is over.

119
00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:13.250
So you

120
00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:14.250
use

121
00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:16.250
a relatively short amount of time,

122
00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:18.250
and gain emotional value.

123
00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:19.250
It is a short-format variety show,

124
00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:20.250
and what it does—

125
00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:21.250
perhaps because it is short—

126
00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:23.250
makes viewers want

127
00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:24.250
things to develop further.

128
00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:25.250
Many viewers feel it is too short, not enough to watch.

129
00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:26.250
You hear that response now.

130
00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:27.250
But I think that is also its distinctive feature:

131
00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:28.250
people have a lot of curiosity.

132
00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:29.250
It feels too short,

133
00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:30.250
so they remain curious about it.

134
00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:33.250
Many of the artists themselves

135
00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:34.250
think it is quite—

136
00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:35.250
really surprising

137
00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:36.250
that something like that was included in the programme.

138
00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:37.250
They did not expect it.

139
00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:38.250
Even they had never imagined

140
00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:39.250
such ordinary things would be included.

141
00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:42.250
Yes. I feel what they achieve

142
00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:45.250
is producers and guests

143
00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:46.250
developing one another. Watching it,

144
00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:47.250
you can feel

145
00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:49.250
a relationship where you are simply filming us.

146
00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:51.250
You could say Daqian recorded their growth.

147
00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:52.250
The guests,

148
00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:53.250
from the heart,

149
00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:54.250
love the programme itself.

150
00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:55.250
They are willing

151
00:03:55.250 --> 00:03:56.250
to help it develop,

152
00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:57.250
promoting it and so on.

153
00:03:57.250 --> 00:03:58.250
It is a process of mutual achievement.

154
00:03:58.250 --> 00:03:59.250
That is how it feels.

155
00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:00.250
And when they choose people,

156
00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:01.250
you can sense

157
00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:02.250
their selected guests

158
00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:04.250
all have distinctive personalities

159
00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:05.250
and are very sincere.

160
00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:06.250
Before selecting guests,

161
00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:08.250
they apparently give them a long,

162
00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:09.250
very long test,

163
00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:10.250
perhaps like an MBTI assessment.

164
00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:11.250
That is how they select

165
00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:12.250
the people they consider suitable.

166
00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:14.250
The people on their programmes

167
00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:15.250
have close relationships with one another,

168
00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:16.250
and those in front of and behind the camera

169
00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:17.250
get on well too.

170
00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:18.250
The result is a very sincere programme.

171
00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:20.250
And,

172
00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:22.250
at the beginning, when they were seeking sponsors,

173
00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:23.250
the audience

174
00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:24.250
understood the situation

175
00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:25.250
and did

176
00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:26.250
whatever promotion they could,

177
00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:27.250
and so on.

178
00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:28.250
For example, many viewers

179
00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:29.250
edit their own videos

180
00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:30.250
to recommend it,

181
00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:31.250
and tell friends around them about it.

182
00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:34.250
I think this is a relationship in which both sides

183
00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:35.250
maintain a sincere attitude

184
00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:36.250
and help one another develop.

